namespace Stride.Rendering.Shadows
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Lets a material shader ask whether it is being run to write a shadow map.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A material's pixel stage is composed into every pass that keeps a pixel shader, and the
    /// shadow caster passes are among them when the material asks for it. Most materials have no
    /// reason to care; one that finds its surface at a cost - a ray walked through a volume, a
    /// parallax search - can settle for a depth good enough for a shadow and skip what a shadow
    /// never reads: the normal, the colour, the last bits of precision.
    ///
    /// The answer is a stream, so it crosses composition boundaries the way every stream does.
    /// Whoever asks must also write it to 0 in the vertex stage, so that it is defined in every
    /// pass; <see cref="ShadowMapCasterPassMarker"/>, mixed into the caster effects, writes the view's flag in
    /// the pixel stage before the material chain runs. A float rather than a bool: a bool cannot
    /// be carried from the vertex stage to the pixel stage.
    ///
    /// A flag per view rather than a constant 1: the caster effect is also what a compositor's
    /// depth-only prepass runs for the camera, and a constant had a material write the depth
    /// it keeps for shadows into the camera's own depth buffer, where the surface it draws
    /// afterwards then failed the depth test wherever the two disagreed.
    /// </remarks>
    shader ShadowMapCasterPassInfo
    {
        cbuffer PerView.ShadowMapView
        {
            /// 1 in a shadow map view, 0 in every other view - the camera's own depth-only
            /// prepass included, which runs the very same caster effect. Written for every
            /// view by ShadowCasterRenderFeature, so it is never stale.
            stage float4 ShadowMapViewFlag;
        }

        stage stream float IsShadowMapCasterPass;
    };
}
